-We also assessed code availability and licensing. Of the 45 projects, 42 link to a public code repository; \cref{tab:licenses} tallies license status per project. Explicit licenses are overwhelmingly permissive (20 MIT, 4 Apache-2.0); 12 projects provide no license file, and 4 link to repositories that were private or removed when checked. Dependency specifications were frequently incomplete, so most outputs are reusable starting points rather than finished tools~\cite{nolte_what_2020}. The license tabulation script is available in the hackathon repository (see Data availability).
+We also assessed code availability and licensing. Of the 45 projects, 42 link to a code repository; \cref{tab:licenses} tallies license status per project. Explicit licenses are overwhelmingly permissive (20 MIT, 4 Apache-2.0); 12 projects provide no license file, and 4 link to repositories that were private or removed when checked. Dependency specifications were frequently incomplete, so most outputs are reusable starting points rather than finished tools~\cite{nolte_what_2020}. The license tabulation script is available in the hackathon repository (see Data availability).
 
 \begin{table}[tbp]
